SM(246,{"n":"Genesis 20","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Genesis 20.mp3","d":3251.5,"s":[[0.24,"Come and study the Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.96,"Father Stephen holds a PhD in Biblical Studies, and is an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[23.36,"So we'll go ahead and get started."],[25.68,"When we get started, in just a moment, we'll be picking up at uh the beginning of Genesis chapter 20."],[38.08,"So last time, uh, by way of catch-up, we we read about we read two connected stories that sometimes maybe in our heads aren't as connected as they are here in the text."],[51.7,"Because the first one is when God and two angels come and receive hospitality from Abraham and Sarah at the Oak of Mamre."],[65.18,"Um hospitality of Abraham, we kind of know that story."],[69.34,"And then story of the destruction of Sodom and Gomorrah."],[73.12,"We kind of know that separate story."],[75.76,"But these, of course, as we saw, are linked in the text."],[80.01,"Because the two angels who come to Sodom and Gomorrah are the two angels who were there with God and Abraham."],[87.61,"Remember, Abraham stayed and talked with God after the meal, tried to say, Hey, if there's 50 righteous people, if there's forty-five, if there's 40, if there's twenty-five."],[97.07,"Um, while the two angels went down into Sodom to get Lot uh and his family."],[103.99,"And we read about the events that unfolded without going back through them all, including what happened with Lot's daughters after the destruction of the cities of the plain."],[119.99,"And that really is where we're picking up now in chapter 20 is after that story."],[127.43,"And as we mentioned last time, the way the story of Abraham is structured, there are these sort of time lapses."],[134.57,"We're not getting like a continuous narrative of every day of his life."],[137.77,"We've seen when it his age has been given."],[140.25,"We've been skipping like sometimes 10 years, sometimes, yeah, like a series of years in between uh stories."],[148.29,"A lot of them are framed in terms of the appearances of God to Abraham."],[155.49,"That's sort of what started the last couple of the narrative sections."],[161.33,"This one isn't going to start that way."],[163.25,"But we're sort of going through these sort of major events in uh in the life of Abraham."],[170.53,"Uh the reason I'm emphasizing that again is um there's a tendency with Genesis to try and act like it's an all-inclusive narrative."],[183.59,"To add up years and and to add up say, okay, this is exactly how old the earth is, etc. et cetera, et cetera, those kind of things."],[190.63,"And it's not pretending to be that."],[194.93,"There are openly these sort of jumps, in time and periods."],[201.34,"And this is this is going to be true later on in the Hebrew scriptures."],[208.56,"And the Old Testament."],[209.68,"And this, of course, is true also in the Gospels."],[211.66,"St. John famously said, if you wrote down everything Christ did and said the world could contain all the books you'd have to write."],[218.48,"So we are on the face of it being selective."],[222.98,"And choosing significant, sort of significant events."],[227.26,"Um the bigger reason why that's important, I don't think refuting the people who, if you want to add up the eight things and say that's exactly how old the earth is, God bless you."],[240.07,"If that's your thing."],[241.64,"But um the more important reason for that is once we're aware of the fact that the text is presenting itself as selecting certain significant events, and putting those selected events in a particular order."],[260.24,"That becomes important to how we read and understand the text."],[269.28,"Means each of these is like, well, okay, so why is this one of the things that was important?"],[274.24,"Why is this one of the important things that was included, as opposed to who knows. how many things that could have been but weren't"],[281.7,"And why are, like we were talking about last time, why are these stories brought together and connected to each other in certain ways or set in parallel to each other?"],[294.11,"Because they didn't have to be."],[295.97,"They could have been in a different order, or one of them could have been left out entirely."],[299.67,"Or right. So there's a deliberateness, and that's part of the meaning of the text."],[305.03,"That's part of the teaching of the text."],[309.31,"Um, and yeah, that becomes important, and years from now, when we get back into the New Testament and do Matthew and Mark, um for example, in the Gospels, that'll become important again."],[325.57,"Of because sometimes people notice, hey, these stories are in a different order in this one gospel than this other gospel."],[333.89,"And so there's a certain group of people who be like, oh, see, it's all lies."],[339.63,"But sensible people are like, oh, well, if these are arranged differently, that means both authors thought they were important, but they're they're interpreting them slightly differently, not necessarily opposed to each other."],[354.68,"And that's why they ordered it a little differently."],[357.72,"Or as another example, we read in places that Christ was healing dozens, if not hundreds of people."],[364.6,"But then there are certain times he heals someone where we get a whole story surrounding it."],[369.72,"Why do we get the whole story of this one when there's all these other healings that are just sort of passed over quickly."],[375.46,"So that's always a question to have when you're reading biblical narrative."],[380.68,"Why this particular episode of the life of Abraham or Isaac or Jacob or Joseph or David or whomever we're reading about."],[388.69,"As opposed to all the others that we've sort of passed over in silence."],[395.89,"So uh that said, unless there are any leftover questions, comments, preposterous political ideologies."],[407.25,"Yes, sir. Yeah, so yeah."],[422.41,"Um so the question was about speaking of negative reviews, but everybody always complains that I don't repeat the questions people ask."],[432.01,"So the question was sort of about how does the hand of God in terms of inspiration work in in what I was just saying."],[441.5,"Um so inspiration is something that we say about the what do you want to say, material, whatever process that happened."],[458.42,"It's not over against the material process that happened."],[462.66,"Here's what I mean by that."],[464.23,"So there are certain folks."],[465.99,"I remember when I was in my undergrad and I first started studying Greek."],[473.83,"And one of my friends who was from a very conservative, shall we say, Southern Baptist Church asked, you know, hey, have you learned any interesting things?"],[486.69,"Like reading the New Testament in the original Greek."],[491.01,"And I said, Yeah, one of the really interesting things I've noticed. is just how different the different authors' writing styles are"],[500.81,"And if you know Greek and you've done anything with the New Testament Greek, you know, it's wildly different."],[505.37,"Like St. John's Greek and St."],[507.13,"Mark's Greek and St."],[508.65,"Paul's Greek are not remotely similar."],[513.42,"And he got really upset when I said that and refused to believe it."],[519.18,"Literally, he said, that's not true."],[522.3,"Like just dogmatically refused to believe it."],[525.42,"Because for him, what he'd been taught is God is the author of the whole thing."],[531.95,"Sort of directly."],[532.91,"At the same time"],[535.31,"Yeah. And so me saying that to him was me claiming that the New Testament, the scriptures were just the work of humans and not of God."],[547.07,"So he was opposing those two ideas."],[549.39,"Like, insofar as humans wrote it, that means God didn't."],[554.51,"And insofar as God wrote it, that means humans didn't."],[559.09,"Like those are at odds with each other."],[561.65,"This is one of the side effects of Plato brain."],[565.01,"Not Plato like you play with Plato like the philosopher."],[571.46,"And therefore of Calvinism, too."],[573.94,"But so yeah, and some people talk almost as if like the biblical author went into some kind of trance and did like automatic writing, you know, and then suddenly like came to and was like, oh, what is this text that I see before me?"],[593.82,"And the scriptures never claim to be that at all."],[600.54,"They never claim to be that."],[602.7,"So the um the key is the orthodox understanding of synergism applies here just like everywhere else."],[614.95,"That that humans working and God working are not opposed to each other."],[620.79,"It's not an either or it's at least potentially a both and."],[628.41,"So we say this about like good works."],[632.87,"Like good works."],[633.91,"If you say if if someone is doing good works, why are they good works?"],[637.59,"Well, because they're doing the works of God."],[641.27,"God is working in and through them."],[643.19,"And so God can call those works good because they're his own works."],[647.29,"That's what makes them good."],[648.89,"But that doesn't mean that the person is purely passive."],[654.59,"The person is also working."],[656.64,"The person is going and feeding the poor."],[659.38,"And expressing the love of God to them."],[661.6,"They're active, they're working."],[663.3,"But it's really God who's working through them."],[666.18,"And it's in Philippians 2 2, I think it's1 and 13."],[667.78,"Paul Sort of the classic summary of that by St."],[673.34,"Continue to work out your salvation with fear and trembling because it's God who is working within you to will and to do."],[707.66,"So what we say is that the Holy Spirit inspired the scriptures in the sense that it was the Holy Spirit who was motivating and guiding the authors of the scriptures, and the later editors of the scriptures, and the people who copied the scriptures down through the ages to get them to us."],[730.84,"It's the same Holy Spirit all the way through."],[732.84,"The Holy Spirit is guiding the church, and with those scriptures, we're being copied and read."],[737.21,"It's Holy Spirit all the way down."],[738.99,"Like that's all the way through from start to finish."],[742.11,"And so uh let's say there's an argument about this, but let's say St."],[750.15,"Paul, St. Paul wrote an epistle to the Laodiceans that we don't have anymore."],[755.09,"The people who want to argue that it's one of the ones we do have or something else."],[756.21,"' But lets just say, on the face of it, as St."],[756.29,"s Paul seem to say, he wrote an epistle to the Laodiceans, and it's not in the Bible."],[767.85,"Okay. Paul knew the difference when he was writing Romans and when he was writing that."],[779.37,"I don't think he had like a little tingle going up his spine or started speaking in tongues or had any kind of special religious experience where he said, This is Scripture, or this other is just a letter."],[794.55,"To a church."],[795.93,"He was doing the same thing both times."],[799.61,"But the church. guided by the same Holy Spirit, was able to discern this thing that St"],[806.77,"Paul wrote, this is inspired, this is scripture."],[809.97,"We need to read this in the church."],[813.25,"This other thing."],[816.93,"Not so much."],[818.34,"Yeah. Um, we don't think that like the apostles had the power to write scripture, so they could just do it whenever they wanted to."],[828.68,"That it's sort of based in their authority."],[830.92,"You hear that kind of talk sometimes too."],[832.92,"Like they're like, oh, it had to be written by an apostle."],[835.16,"Or at least implied."],[836.44,"Yeah, yeah, it had to be written by an apostle."],[838.36,"It's like that's ex post facto."],[840.36,"It's it's those same people will say they don't know who wrote Hebrews."],[843.88,"St Where do you go with that?"],[846.64,"Um so it's not about the authority of the author."],[849.92,"Paul's shopping list was not inspired, it's not scripture."],[855.17,"So it's a quality of those documents that's revealed through the Holy Spirit to the church about those documents."],[864.37,"And this is true across the board."],[866.13,"This is how tradition works in the Orthodox Church."],[869.09,"So, how how do you know what's an ecumenical council and what isn't?"],[875.31,"So they had a robber council in 449, and then they had the camp council of Chalcedon in 451."],[881.73,"I don't think there was like a different atmosphere in the air there, where it glimmered or something."],[887.66,"They thought they were the bishops at both of those thought they were doing the same thing."],[891.18,"They thought they were doing an ecumenical council."],[893.74,"They both said at the end it seemed good to the Holy Spirit and to us."],[897.34,"It's just one of them was right and one of them was wrong."],[900.3,"Well, how do you tell?"],[902.3,"Well, the Holy Spirit in later generations guides the church to see this is the one where the Holy Spirit was working, this other one, no."],[913.36,"But they didn't know at the time, necessarily."],[920.8,"There were people who thought they were doing things guided by the Holy Spirit and were wrong, just like today"],[927.34,"All kinds of people today claim they're doing things guided by the Holy Spirit."],[930.78,"Clearly, at least some of them must be wrong"],[937.3,"So, yeah, so that it's it's a question of, and so that's why tradition works across time."],[942.02,"That's why it's tradition."],[943.06,"It's handed down."],[944.1,"It has to work across time to recognize."],[947.72,"Not everything the church fathers wrote was equally valuable."],[950.68,"We don't have most of the things the church fathers wrote."],[957.48,"The things we have are the things that the Holy Spirit guided the church to."],[963.28,"It's like this needs to be preserved."],[965.4,"This needs to be held on to."],[968.04,"These other things"],[973.81,"So, yeah, so that's that's sort of how we see it."],[976.93,"But the the key difference is, and this is kind of built into Protestantism."],[983.73,"This is a place where Platonism just comes into Protestantism."],[987.17,"The idea that human efforts and the works of God, i.e."],[991.57,"grace, are in some way opposed to one another."],[996.13,"And if it's one, then it's not the other."],[1000.65,"That's a basic mistake."],[1005.23,"That that uh we avoid."],[1010.15,"So, yeah. So, all that is to say, getting back to what I was originally saying, the Holy Spirit was guiding the selection of, hey, what should we record and what should we not record?"],[1017.29,"what And the Holy Spirit was guiding order these things were written in, and the Holy Spirit was guiding all of these things."],[1026.37,"When you're talking about the Torah, we're talking about the Spirit of God working over centuries, if not, well, centuries."],[1035.17,"Where there's editing passes, there's different versions of the text, there's different, and the Holy Spirit is working to guide that whole process."],[1047.25,"Because as we said back in the introduction to the Torah, uh every reason to believe Moses wrote something, but the earliest language we have, the Torah in, is not a language that existed when Moses. was alive"],[1064.73,"And I know there's some mystical Jewish stuff that says he wrote it in later Hebrew at the time."],[1073.77,"But yeah, there's some the Torah, there are some Torah is eternal people in Jewish circles."],[1080.33,"But that dog kind of don't hunt."],[1091.13,"Like Persian Imperial Aramaic."],[1093.47,"Like that's eternal."],[1100.29,"So, yeah. Fair answer?"],[1104.77,"Okay So uh so now, unless there are any further questions, comments, absurd political ideologies to be described."],[1115.93,"We'll go ahead and pick up in uh Genesis chapter 20, verse one."],[1124.41,"Now Abraham journeyed from there to the south."],[1129.37,"From there. So when last we left him, he's camped on Mamre."],[1133.63,"Mamry's a person."],[1135.17,"That big oak is on his land."],[1138.85,"So he journeyed from there."],[1140.69,"He went south from where Mamre lived."],[1145.97,"And dwelt between Kadesh and Sh.ur and sojourned among the Gerarites"],[1160.01,"So uh why did he move?"],[1164.97,"Well, we've already seen right in the conversation between Abraham and Lot, when they had to divide."],[1172.44,"What was the main issue?"],[1174.52,"Their herds, were overgrazing the land."],[1178.62,"So when you live nomadically as a herdsman, you have to move."],[1181.66,"You can't just stop in one place."],[1183.58,"That's why you're a nomad."],[1185.12,"Because you'll overgraze that land."],[1187.44,"Um so the time comes for him to move."],[1190.0,"He moves south, he's between these two cities."],[1191.76,"between the And somewhere those two cities is city of Gerar."],[1198.52,"Gerar's a city."],[1201.64,"And so, as we mentioned before, you have to picture ancient city."],[1206.3,"Walled city, and then there's all the farmland that supports the city surrounding it."],[1212.63,"Because they're growing the food to feed the people in the city."],[1216.13,"Um, and so when it says he lived among the Gerarites, that means he's in the region."],[1222.63,"Around uh the city, but not in the city itself."],[1230.79,"Verse two, get ready for deja vu."],[1234.95,"Then Abraham said of Sarah his wife, She is my sister, for he was afraid to say she is my wife, lest at a given time the men of the city should kill him because of her."],[1246.59,"Now Abimelech, King of Gerar, sent and took Sarah."],[1253.55,"So of us."],[1255.55,"Anyone having dejavu from Egypt?"],[1258.19,"Yeah. Same thing."],[1261.15,"Um, so uh, yeah, unfortunately, I have to say this is yet another place uh where I have to comment on uh 19th century German stupidity."],[1279.71,"I didn't even say our 19th century German friends"],[1279.87,"Um this is one of the evidences that there are multiple sources that have been brought together in the book of Genesis, like the J E D P thing."],[1279.91,"Um I'm being mean about it now."],[1298.79,"Well, this case J-E, really."],[1302.07,"J and E."],[1303.19,"Because, clearly, this is the same story being told twice."],[1308.6,"It's not that he kidding and done the same mistake twice."],[1311.32,"Yeah, no, no, nobody does ever does anything twice."],[1315.08,"Nobody ever sins and then goes back and repeats the same sin."],[1319.56,"This has never happened."],[1321.64,"Especially after you just heard about Law and what he's worried about happening, happening."],[1324.76,"Yes Nor would the Torah want to comment on the fact that people tend to commit the same sin and have the same weakness they struggle with over and over again."],[1342.21,"Not possible. It has to be that, rather, that's ridiculous."],[1348.88,"Here's what makes sense."],[1351.44,"There are two different stories about Abraham doing this with slightly different details, different kings."],[1358.48,"And for some reason, the editor felt like, well, well, gosh, I have to honor both of these versions of the story."],[1366.32,"I can't possibly collapse them into a single story or choose one and get rid of the other since they're so similar."],[1375.16,"I have to keep both."],[1377.4,"I have to keep them in the text."],[1380.04,"And so I'm just gonna put them both in here and hope no one notices."],[1387.48,"And hope no one notices."],[1389.32,"In the centuries of the future, when they praise my literary genius as an editor, I hope they won't also notice this horrible, obvious blunder I'm making."],[1402.52,"That makes sense, but Abraham doing it twice, preposterous."],[1408.68,"This is why I think I'm safe in calling this stupidity."],[1413.93,"Like it's and yes, you could find this in peer-reviewed journals."],[1417.69,"That doesn't make it smart."],[1421.5,"You can get a PhD in almost any field without taking a single logic class."],[1427.5,"Let me just point this out."],[1429.82,"I do not think it should be that way, but it is that way."],[1436.7,"And so what tends to happen is these discussions become academic discussions in academic circles, and they get completely dissociated from reality and common sense."],[1453.07,"Like, they get completely."],[1454.83,"And so it gets to the point where when someone with common sense shows up and says, well, hey, maybe Abraham just did it twice"],[1462.47,"The scholars who have been having this argument about where to split the seams for J and E and all this stuff, look at the go, huh, Rube?"],[1470.51,"Like, you don't you don't know what you're talking about."],[1473.39,"You you haven't read all the literature on, you know, documentary hypothesis."],[1478.51,"And it's just like Earth calling scholars."],[1483.15,"Come in, scholars."],[1484.29,"Um, yeah. So Occam's razor."],[1490.8,"Anybody ever heard of that?"],[1492.08,"Simplest explanation? Probably correct."],[1497.43,"Is that no, Abraham did this a second time?"],[1501.91,"Because he knew he was wrong to do it the first time, but it also worked out okay for him the first time."],[1509.51,"He didn't have a lot of negative consequences."],[1511.35,"Pharaoh had some negative consequences, but Abraham didn't have a lot of."],[1516.23,"Yeah, Abram at the time did not have a lot of negative consequences, even though God made it pretty clear to him."],[1523.99,"Hey, yeah, you need to trust me to protect you, not your own wits."],[1532.11,"But what have we seen all the way through here?"],[1535.57,"What was the last before before the whole Sodom and Gomorrah thing."],[1539.53,"What was the previous conversation between Abraham and God?"],[1543.73,"It was God telling Abraham, you and Sarah are going to have a son."],[1548.21,"Abraham laughing, that was ridiculous, and saying, Well, what about my son Ishmael?"],[1553.81,"I kind of did that myself."],[1560.18,"So there's this problem with God repeatedly calling on Abraham to trust him."],[1569.0,"To trust him."],[1571.56,"And Abraham trying to kind of trust his own wits."],[1578.3,"This is what he sort of struggles with."],[1582.02,"This is important thematically in Abraham's story as we see him develop and grow."],[1586.62,"So him doing this twice is actually really important to this narrative."],[1592.32,"And would not be served by only telling this once."],[1598.08,"And this is an ongoing thing for him that he struggles with."],[1602.48,"And why do I frame it as trust?"],[1604.32,"Well, because God gave him these promises and said, I'm going to fulfill them."],[1616.3,"He gave him these promises."],[1618.14,"And how do you show."],[1620.0,"This gets back to the separation of belief and action."],[1623.3,"How do you show that you actually believe something?"],[1626.94,"It affects how you act."],[1630.42,"So if Abraham really believes that he and Sarah are going to have a son, and that son is going to inherit everything."],[1644.87,"Then why is he scared he's going to get killed by the Guerreroites before that son is conceived?"],[1655.93,"He shouldn't be."],[1658.33,"He should be like, I have plot armor."],[1660.67,"He should be like, there's nothing, nothing can happen to me."],[1665.03,"Because God has promised this is going to happen."],[1666.91,"And so until that happens, I know for sure."],[1673.97,"Doesn't mean you go take stupid risks, you know."],[1677.14,"He doesn't go freestyle rock climbing or something near Gerar, but"],[1682.8,"He should trust that God's gonna protect him."],[1685.76,"And even more so now than before."],[1689.7,"So remember when that first one happened, the first one happened very early."],[1693.24,"That happened a couple decades ago when he first got to the land of Canaan."],[1701.88,"But now it's still kind of an issue, and it shouldn't be anymore."],[1707.88,"God swore that oath."],[1709.42,"That covenant oath in front of him."],[1710.54,"He's seen Lot rescued from Sodom"],[1714.07,"He's, you know, at this point, Abraham should be good, and he's not yet."],[1722.87,"He's not yet."],[1725.43,"So Abimelech's name as the king of Gerar is another one of those uh guys who had a really humble father."],[1736.79,"Uh Abimelech, Avimelech means my father is king."],[1740.63,"So his father, who was king before him, named his son my father is king."],[1745.11,"And so now even though his dad's gone and he's the king, his name is still my father is king."],[1751.85,"So anytime you see like that's not."],[1758.53,"So ab or av is father."],[1761.81,"And the E is my."],[1768.45,"Um, yeah. Yeah, you have a suffix that makes it possessive."],[1778.05,"Like say mine or ours or yours, or yeah."],[1784.29,"Okay. And so Abraham does the same thing."],[1789.01,"Oh man, they're gonna kill me."],[1791.41,"To take my 89-year old wife."],[1795.65,"I'm just saying, Sarah must have been something."],[1800.89,"Um so I gotta say it's she's my sister."],[1806.54,"Um, and kidding aside, this this isn't right, this isn't the modern day."],[1812.8,"This isn't about looks."],[1815.62,"If if this is if this is Abraham's sister, why would they want to marry her?"],[1820.82,"Well, Abraham's rich, Abraham's got all this stuff."],[1824.51,"So you merge your families."],[1827.44,"Um and if it's if he's her husband, the idea is, well, you know, hey, if we off him and marry her, we inherit all this stuff."],[1837.78,"His stuff becomes our stuff."],[1839.68,"Um, so that's really the dynamic."],[1842.68,"It's not just, you know, wow, she must have been an amazing looking 89-year-old."],[1847.66,"It's not kidding aside, that's not that."],[1851.42,"Um, but God came to Abimelech in a dream by night and said to him, Indeed, you are a dead man because of the woman you have taken, for she is a man's wife."],[1867.5,"Okay. So again, God is much more concerned still about protecting Sarah than Abraham is."],[1879.35,"Abraham isn't protecting her."],[1881.99,"Apparently he's fine with with some other dude being his wife as long as, you know, he's safe."],[1893.89,"Which is pretty extreme."],[1897.81,"Pretty extreme. This isn't hospitality like Lot and his daughters."],[1901.09,"This is this is I'm gonna save my own skin, even if it means this is gonna happen to my wife."],[1908.99,"But God steps in and protects her."],[1914.69,"By appearing to this pagan king directly in his dream and telling him he's dead."],[1920.37,"It doesn't say you're gonna die."],[1921.97,"That is an accurate translation."],[1923.89,"It's you are dead."],[1925.99,"You are a dead man."],[1927.03,"Verse 4 For having done this."],[1932.76,"Now Abimelech had not come near her, and he said, Oh Lord, will you destroy an ignorant and just nation?"],[1940.38,"He's like, whoa, whoa, whoa."],[1943.47,"Are you, you know, just why?"],[1947.15,"Like, we're ignorant, we don't know."],[1948.85,"I didn't know."],[1949.57,"I didn't know she was married."],[1950.77,"I didn't, you know."],[1952.74,"Um, but notice what he says there."],[1955.78,"What's the call back there to Abraham talking to God about Sodom?"],[1961.54,"Would you destroy them if there's right, X number of righteous?"],[1965.94,"The word just there is the same word as righteous."],[1970.7,"So Abimelech knows what God's like."],[1976.75,"And wouldn't do this."],[1977.71,"He's basically, I wouldn't do this."],[1979.33,"I would, I wouldn't do, you know."],[1981.01,"Abraham apparently doesn't have a problem with it, but me, pagan me wouldn't do this."],[1986.1,"We need to catch that."],[1988.04,"This this pagan has right now has a sort of a keener sense of morality on this point than Abraham does."],[1993.72,"Because what he's doing is motivated by fear."],[2000.2,"Did he not say to me, She is my sister?"],[2002.92,"And did she not say to me, he is my brother?"],[2005.64,"I did this thing with a clean hearted righteous hands."],[2008.25,"He's like, as far as I knew, everything I was doing was above board."],[2013.43,"Then God said to him in a dream, Yes, I know you did this with a clean heart, and I spared you that you might not sin against me."],[2020.79,"Therefore, I did not allow you to touch her."],[2025.59,"Back to that point."],[2027.27,"Notice what it said."],[2028.47,"He said, It said first he had not touched her."],[2032.23,"Now God says, I didn't let you touch her."],[2035.03,"Now when God says, I didn't let you touch her, does that mean like he tried to and there was a force field?"],[2042.85,"Okay. That God somehow physically stopped him"],[2047.85,"No. It's saying it wasn't just happenstance that you didn't get that far."],[2052.98,"didn't. God's like, hey, no, because of that, I let you"],[2059.0,"Because you were tricked, I didn't let you."],[2061.58,"Fall into that."],[2065.26,"Verse 7. Now therefore, restore the man's wife, for he is a prophet, and he will pray for you, and you shall live."],[2072.06,"But if you do not return her, rest assured, you will die, you and all who are yours."],[2077.58,"You and all who you're yours is not your family."],[2080.22,"He's the king."],[2081.9,"This is you and your city and the surroundings."],[2085.95,"This is Sodom and Gomorhra, yeah, part two, the sequel."],[2089.15,"This time it's personal."],[2090.96,"Like, that's what he's saying."],[2094.04,"And so God tells him, Now you do know."],[2096.82,"So now you have an informed decision to make."],[2099.92,"In terms of what you're going to do."],[2107.76,"Verse 8. So Abimelech rose early in the morning, called all his servants, and told all these things in their hearing."],[2115.04,"And the men were very much afraid."],[2118.13,"So, yes, a god appeared to me in a dream and told me, we're all going to die unless I get this woman back to her husband."],[2126.15,"Then Abimelech called Abraham and said to him, What have you done to us?"],[2130.15,"How have I offended you?"],[2131.87,"That you have brought on me and on my kingdom a great sin?"],[2135.19,"You have done deeds to me that ought not to be done."],[2138.32,"Like, man, like, what did I ever do to you?"],[2142.64,"I've got your God appearing to me in a dream, threatening to wipe out my city, because you lied to me."],[2149.78,"Like, you shouldn't do that to anybody, let alone somebody who never did anything to you."],[2157.16,"So this is this is thrown squarely at Abraham's feet."],[2164.34,"And so notice this isn't just, oh hey, you didn't trust God, you should have trusted God."],[2170.74,"This is your lack of trust in God caused you to be afraid."],[2175.26,"To be afraid of what?"],[2177.38,"Death. And the fear of death, this is picking up a theme from back the expulsion from paradise."],[2186.78,"The fear of death has driven you to sin against this man and his people."],[2209.42,"But we normally see it as sin brings about death."],[2213.76,"Which is true on a personal level."],[2217.72,"What sin in our lives brings about death and destruction, ours and other people's."],[2229.98,"That we see in Genesis, back at Genesis 3, death brings about sin."],[2237.5,"Humanity becomes mortal first."],[2241.9,"And when humanity becomes mortal, the fear of death drives humanity towards sin."],[2250.14,"We also talk about this in Hebrews."],[2251.5,"Hebrews says this explicitly."],[2254.94,"And so Abraham here is illustrating this point."],[2261.1,"The fear of death caused him to sin against this man."],[2264.78,"To almost get this man and a whole lot of other relatively innocent people killed."],[2269.42,"Because he's worried about saving his own skin"],[2275.22,"And so this is an important perspective in Genesis and throughout the Torah about what leads us into sin."],[2285.4,"Lack of trust in God leads to fear."],[2289.32,"Fear leads to sin."],[2292.92,"Fear of death in particular."],[2295.5,"We think we become greedy because we think, well, if I could just get enough money put away, if I could just right, then I'll be safe, I'll be secure, all right?"],[2308.86,"Or we become arrogant."],[2310.96,"We become angry."],[2313.25,"Why do you think people get so angry while they're driving in particular?"],[2318.93,"They're afraid they're gonna have an accident."],[2322.13,"Yeah, they're afraid they're gonna have an accident."],[2324.05,"Someone comes and cuts you off, and you're almost in an accident."],[2326.69,"They could have killed you, and so you get so angry at them that you could kill them in response."],[2337.59,"So this is an important insight here."],[2343.75,"That G maybe justifies telling about the second time Abraham did this."],[2359.23,"Verse 10, again, Abimelech said to Abraham, What possessed you to do this thing?"],[2364.37,"And that's a very literal translation of the Greek, by the way."],[2367.89,"What possessed you?"],[2369.89,"What took hold of you?"],[2371.17,"What took control that would cause you to do this?"],[2377.89,"Abraham replied, Because I thought, surely the worship of God is not in this place, and they will kill me on account of my wife."],[2388.29,"But indeed, she truly is my sister."],[2390.53,"She is the daughter of my father, but not of my mother, and she became my wife."],[2398.29,"Are we following the excuse making?"],[2403.14,"So he said, Oh, the worship of God is not in this place, so they're going to be bad people."],[2408.18,"Looks to me like Abimelech is listening to God on this one, and Abraham may be not so much."],[2417.15,"And then he goes to, well, technically, she is kind of my sister."],[2425.07,"She is my half sister, but yeah, we are married."],[2429.39,"So technically, I didn't lie."],[2432.03,"But uh. So it came to pass when God caused me to wander from my father's house."],[2443.79,"When God caused me, God brought me here, so now it's kind of God's fault."],[2452.85,"We're supposed to be reminded of Genesis 3."],[2456.07,"God shows up."],[2458.23,"Was it me?"],[2459.11,"Is this woman you put here with me?"],[2461.03,"Wasn't me, it was this snake."],[2463.43,"It wasn't me, it was right."],[2465.83,"Pass it down the line."],[2470.63,"I said to her, This righteousness shall you do for me."],[2474.63,"This righteousness shall you do for me."],[2478.55,"In every place we enter, say of me, he is my brother."],[2485.91,"So number one, he's calling this. righteousness"],[2491.87,"And number two, he's saying, Yeah, we do this all the time."],[2495.73,"Every place we get."],[2496.95,"So he's saying he's done this more than once."],[2505.43,"Verse 14, then Abimelech took one thousand pieces of silver, sheep, oxen, and male and female servants, and gave them to Abraham, and he restored Sarah his wife to him."],[2515.67,"Thus Abimelech said to Abraham, See, my land is before you, dwell where it pleases you."],[2521.11,"Then to Sarah he said, Behold, I have given your brother a thousand your brother."],[2525.36,"Catch the irony."],[2528.0,"Behold, I've given your brother a thousand pieces of silver, which will vindicate. your honor before all those with you, but tell the truth in all things"],[2544.12,"It says that she is evil."],[2549.72,"Well, that's the I think that's the rebuke."],[2553.56,"Is him saying, but you need to start telling the truth."],[2557.96,"You need to stop lying."],[2559.96,"Would you read your version again?"],[2562.36,"This is based on the Greek."],[2564.12,"That Abimelech, the last thing he says is, but tell the truth in all things."],[2569.8,"Yeah. You gotta stop doing this."],[2573.72,"You gotta stop lying for him."],[2578.16,"So Abraham prayed to God, and God healed Abimelech his wife and his female servants."],[2583.12,"Then they bore children, for the Lord closed up all the wombs of the house of Abimelech because of Sarah, Abraham's wife."],[2594.57,"That sounds like it was uh an extended period of time after they were marrying, which makes the whole comment of no no, I kept you away from her more meaningful than oh, you just missed a night."],[2606.81,"Yeah, no, this isn't like the next day."],[2609.05,"Yeah Yeah, yeah, yeah."],[2610.73,"Yeah. Why did God wait so long to do the dream?"],[2616.57,"He's giving Abraham a chance to fess up."],[2622.11,"But when the chips were down, he was going to protect Sarah."],[2628.08,"So Sarah gets straightened out on this."],[2630.64,"Abraham's still a work in progress."],[2635.33,"So we have to remember that um part of what's going on here as we go through Abraham's life is we're seeing an arc."],[2644.53,"Where he starts out and who he becomes at the end."],[2649.58,"And we're still in the midst of that arc."],[2654.08,"The it is very important that uh the scriptures especially do not present the lives of even saints as being perfect."],[2676.92,"And that's when we see their life described."],[2679.08,"I mean, like Enoch, okay, is kind of presented as perfect, but that's just because he's he's in one verse."],[2688.49,"Like we aren't told anything."],[2690.41,"But I mean, even Noah, Noah is described, he's righteous among his generation, and then he ends up getting drunk, and there's the whole thing with his family falling apart."],[2698.27,"So, I mean, he's not perfect."],[2701.6,"Um there is a difference between being a holy person, becoming a saint, and being perfect all along."],[2716.28,"There are a few individuals."],[2718.45,"The Theotokos, St."],[2720.61,"John the Forerunner."],[2723.19,"There are others who are perfect in the sense of God keeps them from entering into active sin."],[2734.31,"But most of the saints, even the ones who are described as perfect, so for example, David is described as perfect."],[2745.27,"David, after his death, when the prophet goes to Jeroboam, he says, If you do what my servant David did, and you keep all of my commandments and walk in all of my ways, I too will get I will give you an eternal kingdom, too."],[2763.63,"And this is with, you know, a few pages back, Bathsheba, Absalom, all the things."],[2776.56,"David is described as perfect not because there was never an issue with sin in his life ever."],[2784.42,"He's perfect in the sense that over the course of his life, God brings him to maturity and completion and perfection."],[2794.54,"And part of that is repentance."],[2798.78,"As we're reading in the Torah, part of the Torah, part of the law, part of the commandments."],[2805.48,"You're literally commanded to repent."],[2810.28,"So not only can you sin, repent, offer sacrifices, and still be said to keep the whole law, technically, you can't keep the whole law without sinning."],[2827.64,"Because you can't repent."],[2831.04,"So the Torah expects people to sin."],[2836.4,"It presents how to repent from that, how to be restored from that"],[2837.24,"It doesn't say that's good."],[2838.6,"It doesn't say that's okay, don't worry about it."],[2843.8,"How to more importantly grow closer to God through that experience."],[2849.8,"But it doesn't say, you know, oh, nope, you blew it."],[2855.96,"We'll let you back in, but you will never ever again be able to say that you're a saint or holy or kept the law, kept the Torah."],[2864.2,"No, quite the opposite."],[2869.64,"And this is not something that changes in the New Testament."],[2872.6,"St. Paul says that while he was still a Pharisee persecuting the church, he was blameless according to the Torah."],[2882.24,"Before he was a Christian."],[2886.4,"He was blameless."],[2887.76,"He was perfect according to the Torah."],[2890.24,"And he doesn't have a problem saying that as a Christian now, when he says it."],[2897.02,"Because, yeah, he sinned."],[2899.1,"And he repented."],[2900.22,"And he went and offered sacrifices."],[2902.14,"And when he realized, when Christ appeared to him, and he realized the grievous sins he had been committing against Christ's church, he repented of that too."],[2912.06,"He went out of the desert for years."],[2917.58,"And repented of what he had done."],[2919.58,"done. And when he was repenting, that sin, he was again blameless, according to the Torah"],[2928.58,"So there is an approach to the scriptures, which I'm not taking."],[2934.82,"That's why I'm bringing this up with Abraham."],[2937.94,"Which is Abraham is a righteous man."],[2941.7,"Abraham is a prototypical saint."],[2944.5,"Therefore, when we read the text and it looks like he's doing something wrong."],[2950.42,"We have to figure out a way that he's not."],[2956.63,"We have to figure out a way to say, oh no, no, no, no, this he's not sitting by lying and doing this."],[2962.07,"Uh uh. And not only is that unnecessary and misguided, but that takes you away from a big part of what the text is trying to teach us."],[2977.68,"That Abraham, as Abram and Ur, was an outstanding individual in some way."],[2983.22,"He was chosen by God for some reason, known only to God, by him and not any of the other hundreds of thousands of people alive in the world at the time."],[2993.54,"God knows. But he would he was chosen for this."],[3001.86,"And then over the course of his life, he comes to know God."],[3007.55,"He comes to, and he comes ultimately to perfection."],[3012.59,"And this is the flip side of one of the things when we're reading St."],[3016.95,"Paul's epistles that I really hammered on."],[3021.03,"Because it's very important to understanding St."],[3023.59,"Paul. It's in the second half of his epistles that our Protestant friends don't read as much."],[3028.79,"They like the first half of the theological parts, and they read less of the second half."],[3033.91,"Because that's usually him telling people to do things."],[3053.19,"Paul sees when he reads the Torah and reads about Abraham, this is. how he sees Abraham"],[3061.71,"That's why he holds him up in Galatians and Romans as an example for us."],[3076.99,"And sinned sometimes and messed up."],[3080.01,"But came back and learned and grew, and by the end of his life reached that maturity, that perfection, became the person God created him to be, which is our goal."],[3094.41,"Which is our goal."],[3095.21,"He's an example."],[3097.13,"And this underlies the whole concept of sainthood in the Orthodox Church."],[3101.13,"Why do you identify a particular person as a saint?"],[3105.81,"Because this is a person who shows us how that kind of Christian life is lived."],[3115.25,"And different saints in different ways."],[3118.45,"But shows us how that kind of life is lived."],[3122.53,"And a lot of them, as it says on the Sunday of the, I believe it's the Sunday of the Fefathers."],[3128.29,"Might be the Sunday of the genealogy, but I think it's the Sunday of the Forefathers before the Nativity of Christ."],[3133.57,"When it's describing the forefathers, the Old Testament says, many of which sinned greatly, but also repented greatly."],[3146.13,"And so that's important"],[3149.93,"It's important that we see Abraham's humanity."],[3154.41,"He's not a superhero."],[3155.77,"He didn't come from Krypton."],[3157.83,"He's not different from you and me."],[3163.67,"He didn't start out different than you and me."],[3166.87,"But the life he led led him to a certain place, a place that we're wanting to head toward too."],[3174.23,"And so reading his story as Arch."],[3178.21,"And so I I want to I'm emphasizing this here because this particular story, we won't understand."],[3187.25,"This isn't just the story of Abraham messing up again."],[3190.45,"This certainly isn't just like, oh well, Garsh I gotta keep both these stories in here for some reason."],[3196.94,"Um this is part of the ark."],[3201.26,"We tend to read the scriptures in terms of stories."],[3206.23,"We read this story, and then we read this story, and then we read this story."],[3209.75,"Um, but there's also an arc."],[3213.27,"There's an arc here with Abraham of these stories forming this movement and this progress and the story of his life."],[3221.99,"And this is an important moment in that to focus on."],[3229.11,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[3236.51,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[3241.79,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":23.36,"lv":2,"h":"Recap: two linked stories","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Picking up at beginning of <b>Genesis 20</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Last time: two <b>connected</b> stories</li><li class=\"d1\">God and two angels receive <b>hospitality</b> at the Oak of Mamre</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>destruction of Sodom and Gomorrah</b> - we think of them as separate</li><li class=\"d0\">Linked in the text: the <b>two angels</b> in Sodom are the two with God and Abraham</li><li class=\"d1\">Abraham stayed talking with God: 50, 45, 40...</li><li class=\"d1\">angels went to get <b>Lot</b>; also Lot's daughters after the destruction</li><li class=\"d0\">Chapter 20 picks up <b>after</b> that</li></ul>"},{"t":127.43,"lv":2,"h":"Genesis is selective, not all-inclusive","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Abraham's story has <b>time lapses</b> - not every day of his life</li><li class=\"d1\">skipping sometimes <b>10 years</b> between stories</li><li class=\"d1\">many framed by <b>appearances of God</b> - this one won't start that way</li><li class=\"d0\">Tendency to treat Genesis as <b>all-inclusive</b></li><li class=\"d1\">add up years: \"exactly how old the earth is\" - \"God bless you, if that's your thing\"</li><li class=\"d1\">it's <b>not pretending</b> to be that - open jumps in time</li><li class=\"d0\">Same later in the Hebrew Scriptures and the Gospels</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>John</b>: if you wrote down everything Christ did, the world couldn't contain the books</li><li class=\"d0\">Face of it: <b>selecting significant events</b></li></ul>"},{"t":241.64,"lv":3,"h":"Why this episode? Order and arrangement","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Text presents itself as selecting events <b>in a particular order</b> → matters for how we read</li><li class=\"d0\">Questions to ask</li><li class=\"d1\">why is <b>this</b> one included, out of all that could have been?</li><li class=\"d1\">why are stories <b>brought together</b> and set in parallel (like last time)?</li><li class=\"d1\">could have been in a different order or left out → <b>deliberateness is part of the teaching</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Matters again in the <b>Gospels</b> (Matthew, Mark, years from now)</li><li class=\"d1\">stories in different order in different gospels → skeptics: \"it's all lies\"</li><li class=\"d1\">sensible: both authors thought them important, <b>interpreting slightly differently</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Christ healing dozens if not hundreds - why a whole story around <b>this one</b>?</li><li class=\"d0\">Always ask: why this episode of <b>Abraham, Isaac, Jacob, Joseph, David</b> as opposed to those passed over in silence</li></ul>"},{"t":395.89,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: Inspiration and human authors","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: how does the <b>hand of God / inspiration</b> work with what I said? (\"everybody complains I don't repeat questions\")</li><li class=\"d0\">Inspiration is <b>not over against</b> the material human process</li><li class=\"d0\">Undergrad story: learning <b>Greek</b>, Southern Baptist friend</li><li class=\"d1\">noted how different the authors' styles are: <b>John, Mark, Paul</b> \"not remotely similar\"</li><li class=\"d1\">he got upset, \"dogmatically refused\" - God is author directly</li><li class=\"d1\">took it as claiming scripture is <b>just human</b> - opposing the two: if humans wrote it, God didn't, and vice versa</li><li class=\"d0\">Side effect of \"<b>Plato brain</b>\" - and of <b>Calvinism</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Some talk as if the author did <b>automatic writing</b> in a trance</li><li class=\"d1\">scriptures <b>never claim</b> that</li></ul>"},{"t":602.7,"lv":3,"h":"Synergism: God works, we work","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Key: <b>Orthodox synergism</b> applies here like everywhere</li><li class=\"d1\">humans working and God working - not either/or but <b>both/and</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Like <b>good works</b></li><li class=\"d1\">good because they are <b>God's works</b>, God working in and through</li><li class=\"d1\">person not passive - going and feeding the poor, expressing God's love</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Philippians 2:12-13</b>: \"work out your salvation with fear and trembling, for it is God working in you to will and to do\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Holy Spirit inspired scripture by <b>motivating and guiding</b></li><li class=\"d1\">authors, <b>later editors</b>, those who <b>copied</b> it down the ages</li><li class=\"d1\">\"Holy Spirit all the way down\" - same Spirit guiding the church</li></ul>"},{"t":742.11,"lv":2,"h":"The Laodiceans letter and discernment","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Say Paul wrote an <b>epistle to the Laodiceans</b> we don't have</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul knew no difference between writing it and writing <b>Romans</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no tingle up the spine, tongues, special experience: \"this is Scripture\"</li><li class=\"d1\">same thing both times</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Church</b>, guided by same Spirit, <b>discerned</b>: this is inspired, read it in church; the other - \"not so much\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not \"apostles had the power to write scripture whenever they wanted\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"had to be written by an apostle\" = <b>ex post facto</b></li><li class=\"d1\">same people say they don't know who wrote <b>Hebrews</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not authority of the author: <b>Paul's shopping list</b> not scripture</li><li class=\"d1\">quality of the documents <b>revealed through the Spirit to the church</b></li></ul>"},{"t":864.37,"lv":3,"h":"Same pattern: tradition and councils","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Across the board - <b>how tradition works</b> in the Orthodox Church</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Robber council 449</b> vs <b>Chalcedon 451</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no different atmosphere, glimmer in the air</li><li class=\"d1\">bishops at both thought they were doing an ecumenical council; both \"seemed good to the Holy Spirit and to us\"</li><li class=\"d1\">one right, one wrong → <b>later generations</b>, guided by the Spirit, see which</li><li class=\"d0\">People then and today claim Spirit guidance - \"at least some must be wrong\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Why it's <b>tradition</b> - handed down, works across time</li><li class=\"d1\">not everything the fathers wrote was equally valuable; <b>most of it we don't have</b></li><li class=\"d1\">what we have = what the Spirit guided the church to preserve</li></ul>"},{"t":973.81,"lv":2,"h":"Platonism in Protestantism; the Torah's long formation","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Key difference, <b>built into Protestantism</b>: <b>Platonism</b> comes in</li><li class=\"d1\">human efforts and God's works (<b>grace</b>) opposed - \"if it's one, not the other\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"a basic mistake\" we avoid</li><li class=\"d0\">Back to original point: Spirit guided <b>what to record, in what order</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Torah</b>: Spirit working over <b>centuries</b></li><li class=\"d1\">editing passes, different versions of the text</li><li class=\"d0\">As in the <b>intro to the Torah</b>: every reason to believe <b>Moses wrote something</b></li><li class=\"d1\">but earliest language we have is <b>not a language that existed</b> in Moses' time</li><li class=\"d1\">\"Torah is eternal\" Jewish view - Moses wrote in later Hebrew: \"that dog don't hunt\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"Persian Imperial Aramaic - that's eternal?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Fair answer?\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1115.93,"lv":2,"h":"20:1-2 - Abraham at Gerar","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>20:1</b> - Abraham journeys <b>south</b> from Mamre</li><li class=\"d1\">Mamre is a <b>person</b>; the oak is on his land</li><li class=\"d0\">Dwells between <b>Kadesh and Shur</b>, sojourns among the <b>Gerarites</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Why move? Remember the split with Lot: <b>herds overgrazing</b></li><li class=\"d1\">nomadic herdsman has to move or overgraze</li><li class=\"d0\">City of <b>Gerar</b> between those two</li><li class=\"d1\">picture <b>walled city</b> + surrounding farmland feeding it</li><li class=\"d1\">\"among the Gerarites\" = in the region, not in the city</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>20:2</b> - \"get ready for <b>deja vu</b>\": \"She is my sister\" - afraid they'd <b>kill him</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Abimelech king of Gerar</b> sends and <b>takes Sarah</b></li><li class=\"d1\">anyone having déjà vu from <b>Egypt</b>?</li></ul>"},{"t":1261.15,"lv":2,"h":"Documentary hypothesis and the doublet","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Another comment on \"19th century German stupidity\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Evidence of <b>multiple sources</b> - <b>J E D P</b>; here J and E</li><li class=\"d1\">\"clearly the same story told twice\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Sarcasm: nobody ever <b>sins and repeats the same sin</b>, especially after hearing Lot's worry</li><li class=\"d1\">Torah wouldn't comment that people have the <b>same weakness over and over</b> - \"not possible\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"What makes sense\": two stories, different details and kings</li><li class=\"d1\">editor must honor both, can't collapse or choose</li><li class=\"d1\">\"put them both in and hope no one notices\" the \"horrible obvious blunder\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Abraham doing it twice, preposterous\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1408.68,"lv":3,"h":"Academia and common sense","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Calls it stupidity - \"you could find this in <b>peer-reviewed journals</b>; doesn't make it smart\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"You can get a PhD in almost any field without taking a single <b>logic class</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Academic discussions get <b>dissociated from reality and common sense</b></li><li class=\"d1\">someone with common sense says \"maybe Abraham just did it twice\"</li><li class=\"d1\">scholars arguing where to split seams: \"huh, Rube?\" - \"Earth calling scholars\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Occam's razor</b>: simplest explanation</li></ul>"},{"t":1497.43,"lv":2,"h":"Why Abraham did it twice - trust","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Abraham did it again</li><li class=\"d1\">knew it was wrong the first time, but it <b>worked out OK</b> for him</li><li class=\"d1\">Pharaoh had consequences; Abram didn't</li><li class=\"d1\">God made clear: <b>trust me to protect you, not your own wits</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Previous conversation with God: you and Sarah will have a son</li><li class=\"d1\">Abraham laughed; \"what about <b>Ishmael</b>\" - did it himself</li><li class=\"d0\">Repeated pattern: God calls Abraham to <b>trust</b>; Abraham trusts his <b>own wits</b></li><li class=\"d1\">thematically important to his development → <b>twice is essential</b>, once wouldn't serve</li><li class=\"d0\">Why frame as trust? God gave <b>promises</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"how do you show you believe something?\" → it <b>affects how you act</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1630.42,"lv":3,"h":"Plot armor","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">If he believes Sarah will bear a son who inherits everything</li><li class=\"d1\">why afraid of being killed before the son is conceived?</li><li class=\"d1\">he should have \"<b>plot armor</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not taking stupid risks - \"no freestyle rock climbing near Gerar\"</li><li class=\"d0\">First time was <b>decades ago</b>, early in Canaan</li><li class=\"d1\">now he has the <b>covenant oath</b> sworn before him, has seen <b>Lot rescued</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"should be good, and he's not yet\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1725.43,"lv":2,"h":"20:2-3 - Abimelech and the dream","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Abimelech</b> = \"<b>my father is king</b>\" - name from his father, still named so as king</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>ab/av</b> = father; <b>-i</b> = my (possessive suffix)</li><li class=\"d0\">Abraham again: \"they'll kill me to take my <b>89-year-old wife</b>\" - \"Sarah must have been something\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not about looks</li><li class=\"d1\">if sister: <b>merge families</b>, Abraham rich</li><li class=\"d1\">if husband: kill him, marry her, <b>his stuff becomes our stuff</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>20:3</b> - God comes to Abimelech <b>in a dream by night</b>: \"you are a <b>dead man</b> because of the woman, she is a man's wife\"</li><li class=\"d1\">accurate: \"you <b>are</b> dead,\" not \"will die\"</li><li class=\"d0\">God <b>more concerned to protect Sarah than Abraham</b> is</li><li class=\"d1\">Abraham protecting himself - not like Lot's hospitality</li><li class=\"d1\">God steps in, appearing to a <b>pagan king</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1927.03,"lv":2,"h":"20:4-6 - A just nation, a clean heart","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>20:4</b> - Abimelech had not come near her: \"Will you destroy an <b>ignorant and just nation</b>?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"I didn't know she was married\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Callback to Abraham and Sodom: <b>just</b> = same word as <b>righteous</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"pagan me wouldn't do this\" - Abimelech knows what God is like</li><li class=\"d1\">pagan has <b>keener morality</b> here than Abraham, who acts from <b>fear</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>20:5</b> - \"Did he not say, she is my sister?\" - clean heart, hands</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>20:6</b> - God: \"I know... I <b>spared you</b> that you might not sin against me; I did not allow you to touch her\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not a force field - <b>not happenstance</b></li><li class=\"d1\">because you were tricked, I didn't let you fall into it</li></ul>"},{"t":2065.26,"lv":2,"h":"20:7-10 - Restore her, he is a prophet","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>20:7</b> - \"restore the man's wife, for he is a <b>prophet</b>, he will pray for you\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"you and all who are yours\" = not family: the <b>king, city, surroundings</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"<b>Sodom and Gomorrah part two, the sequel</b>... this time it's personal\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Now an <b>informed decision</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>20:8</b> - rises early, tells servants; men very afraid</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>20:9</b> - \"What have you done to us?... a <b>great sin</b>?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"what did I ever do to you?\" - God threatening my city because you lied</li><li class=\"d0\">Thrown at Abraham's feet - not just \"trust God\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>fear of death</b> made you sin against this man and his people</li></ul>"},{"t":2177.38,"lv":3,"h":"Fear of death leads to sin","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Theme from the <b>expulsion from paradise</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Usually: <b>sin brings death</b> - true personally</li><li class=\"d1\">but <b>Genesis 3</b>: <b>death brings about sin</b></li><li class=\"d1\">humanity mortal first → <b>fear of death drives us to sin</b></li><li class=\"d1\">also stated explicitly in <b>Hebrews</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Abraham illustrates: almost got an innocent man and many others killed to <b>save his own skin</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Perspective across Genesis and the Torah</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>lack of trust → fear → sin</b>, fear of death in particular</li><li class=\"d1\">greed: \"if I just put enough away, I'll be safe\"</li><li class=\"d1\">arrogance, anger: <b>driving</b> - afraid of accident, cut off → angry enough to \"kill them in response\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Maybe justifies telling the second time</li></ul>"},{"t":2359.23,"lv":2,"h":"20:10-13 - Abraham's excuses","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>20:10</b> - \"What possessed you?\" - literal; \"what took hold of you, took control\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>20:11</b> - \"surely the <b>worship of God is not in this place</b>, they'll kill me\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Abimelech listening to God more than Abraham</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>20:12</b> - \"she truly is my sister... daughter of my father, not my mother\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"technically I didn't lie\" - half sister, but married</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>20:13</b> - \"when <b>God caused me to wander</b> from my father's house\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"God brought me here, so now it's God's fault\"</li><li class=\"d1\">reminds of <b>Genesis 3</b>: \"the woman you put here with me\"... \"the snake\" - <b>pass it down the line</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"This <b>righteousness</b> you shall do for me: say he is my brother\"</li><li class=\"d1\">calling it <b>righteousness</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"every place we enter\" → done it <b>more than once</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2505.43,"lv":2,"h":"20:14-16 - Gifts and rebuke","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>20:14</b> - Abimelech gives <b>1,000 pieces of silver</b>, sheep, oxen, servants; restores Sarah</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>20:15</b> - \"my land is before you, dwell where it pleases you\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>20:16</b> - to Sarah: \"I have given <b>your brother</b> 1,000 silver\" - \"catch the irony\"</li><li class=\"d1\">vindicates your honor before all</li><li class=\"d1\">\"<b>tell the truth in all things</b>\" - the rebuke (Greek-based version)</li><li class=\"d1\">\"stop lying for him\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2578.16,"lv":2,"h":"20:17-18 - Abraham prays; wombs closed","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>20:17</b> - Abraham prays; God <b>heals Abimelech</b>, wife, female servants → they bore children</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>20:18</b> - Lord had <b>closed all the wombs</b> of Abimelech's house because of Sarah</li><li class=\"d0\">Sounds like an <b>extended period</b></li><li class=\"d1\">makes \"I kept you from her\" more meaningful than \"you just missed a night\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: why did God wait so long to do the dream?</li><li class=\"d1\">A: giving Abraham a <b>chance to fess up</b></li><li class=\"d1\">but when chips were down, he <b>protected Sarah</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Sarah gets straightened out... Abraham's still a <b>work in progress</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2635.33,"lv":2,"h":"Saints are not presented as perfect","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Abraham's life = an <b>arc</b>: where he starts, who he becomes</li><li class=\"d0\">Scriptures <b>don't present even saints as perfect</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Enoch</b> seems perfect - only one verse</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Noah</b>: righteous in his generation, then <b>drunk</b>, family falling apart</li><li class=\"d0\">Holy person / saint ≠ perfect all along</li><li class=\"d1\">a few kept from active sin: the <b>Theotokos</b>, <b>St. John the Forerunner</b>, others</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>David</b> described as perfect</li><li class=\"d1\">prophet to <b>Jeroboam</b>: do what my servant David did → eternal kingdom</li><li class=\"d1\">yet <b>Bathsheba, Absalom</b></li><li class=\"d1\">perfect = God brings him to <b>maturity and completion</b> over a lifetime</li><li class=\"d1\">part of that is <b>repentance</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2798.78,"lv":3,"h":"The Torah commands repentance","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Part of the law: you're <b>commanded to repent</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Sin, repent, offer sacrifices - still said to keep the whole law</li><li class=\"d1\">technically <b>can't keep the whole law without sinning</b> - can't repent</li><li class=\"d0\">Torah <b>expects people to sin</b></li><li class=\"d1\">shows how to <b>repent and be restored</b></li><li class=\"d1\">how to grow <b>closer to God</b> through it</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"you blew it, never again a saint\" - quite the opposite</li><li class=\"d0\">Same in NT: <b>Paul</b> as Pharisee persecuting church: <b>blameless</b> according to Torah</li><li class=\"d1\">sinned, repented, offered sacrifices</li><li class=\"d1\">on realizing sins against Christ's church: repented again, <b>years in the desert</b></li><li class=\"d1\">repenting - again blameless</li></ul>"},{"t":2928.58,"lv":2,"h":"Not reading Abraham as flawless","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">An approach he is <b>not taking</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Abraham = righteous, <b>prototypical saint</b></li><li class=\"d1\">so when he seems to do wrong, <b>figure out how he's not</b> - \"he's not sitting by lying\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Unnecessary, misguided → takes you away from what the text teaches</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Abram in Ur</b>: outstanding in some way, <b>chosen by God</b> for reasons known to God, not hundreds of thousands of others</li><li class=\"d1\">over his life he <b>comes to know God</b>, comes to <b>perfection</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Flip side of what he hammered on in <b>Paul's epistles</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>second half</b> of the epistles - Protestant friends read less; \"usually him telling people to do things\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul reads the Torah and sees Abraham this way</li><li class=\"d1\">holds him up in <b>Galatians and Romans</b> as example</li><li class=\"d1\">sinned, messed up, <b>came back, learned, grew</b>, reached maturity: <b>our goal</b></li></ul>"},{"t":3097.13,"lv":2,"h":"Sainthood and the arc of Abraham","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Underlies <b>sainthood in the Orthodox Church</b></li><li class=\"d1\">a saint shows <b>how that kind of Christian life is lived</b>, in different ways</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Sunday of the Forefathers</b> (before Nativity): many \"<b>sinned greatly, but also repented greatly</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Important to see Abraham's <b>humanity</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"not a superhero, didn't come from <b>Krypton</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">didn't start different from us; his life led to a place we want to head too</li><li class=\"d0\">This story is <b>not</b> just \"Abraham messing up again\" or \"gotta keep both stories\"</li><li class=\"d0\">We read scriptures as <b>stories</b>, one then another - but also an <b>arc</b></li><li class=\"d1\">stories form <b>movement and progress</b> in his life</li><li class=\"d1\">an important moment in the arc</li></ul>"}]});
